Chapter: EcdsaCertTest
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter X509Chain
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 An ECDSA-signed certificate nobody here minted, checked here.

 The three certificates github.com served on 2026-07-26. The leaf is
 CN=github.com with a P-256 key, signed with ecdsa-with-SHA256 by
 "Sectigo Public Server Authentication CA DV E36", whose own key is also
 P-256; that intermediate is in turn signed with ecdsa-with-SHA384 by a
 root on P-384. One chain therefore carries three cases at once, and it
 carries them because a real hierarchy is built that way, not because
 somebody arranged a fixture to be interesting.

 Until 2026-07-28 those three read "the signature we can check, the curve
 we cannot, and the digest we have not implemented", and this file's
 recorded answers were the shape of what was missing: the root parsed as
 `ecdsa-other` with can-verify False, and `inter signed by root` was
 False. All three are implemented now and both lines changed -- the root
 is `ecdsa-p384`, and the intermediate verifies against it under SHA-384.
 **The chain that motivated the work now checks end to end**, which is the
 only reason to believe the P-384 and SHA-384 code does what its own
 unit tests say it does.

 The positive was confirmed against TWO independent implementations
 before any of the code below was written: OpenSSL 3.2.4, verifying the
 leaf against the intermediate as a partial trust anchor, and .NET's
 ECDsa on Windows CNG, verifying the sliced tbsCertificate directly. Both
 also reject every perturbation here. A transcription error in these
 fixtures has somewhere to show up, which a single-oracle check could not
 offer.

 THESE CERTIFICATES WILL EXPIRE AND THIS TEST WILL NOT NOTICE, on the
 same reasoning RealCertTest sets out: a signature does not expire, and
 this chapter asks only whether these bytes were signed by that key.

 We say:

